// Cloudflare Workers Builds.
//
// Only read when this repository is connected to a Worker. Cloudflare Pages
// ignores a Wrangler file that has no `pages_build_output_dir`, using it for
// local development only, so shipping this changes nothing for a site already
// deployed there. Netlify and Vercel never look at it.
//
// Pages does not ignore it quietly, though. Every Pages build of a site made
// from this theme now logs:
//
//   Found wrangler.json file. Reading build configuration...
//   A Wrangler configuration file was found but it does not appear to be valid.
//   Did you mean to use wrangler.toml to configure Pages? If so, then make sure
//   the file is valid and contains the `pages_build_output_dir` property.
//   Skipping file and continuing.
//
// That is expected and harmless. "Skipping file and continuing" is Pages doing
// exactly what the paragraph above says it does, and the build then succeeds.
// It is written down here because somebody debugging an unrelated Pages failure
// will otherwise spend an hour on a line that says "does not appear to be
// valid".
//
// Adding `pages_build_output_dir` to silence it is not the fix, and that was
// tested rather than assumed. With the key present, `wrangler deploy` warns
// "It seems that you have run `wrangler deploy` on a Pages project ...
// Proceeding will likely produce unwanted results" and stops for a confirmation
// that only a non-interactive fallback answers. Worse, the key is what turns
// this file from local-development-only into the source of truth for a Pages
// project's configuration, which would silently override the dashboard settings
// of every Pages site already deployed from this theme. A log line is much the
// cheaper of the two.
//
// The two values below that fail *silently* when they are wrong are checked
// against the build by `test/wrangler.test.ts`.
{
  // This has to match the Worker's name in the Cloudflare dashboard. Workers
  // Builds fails the build when the two disagree, so if you named your Worker
  // something else, change this line rather than renaming the Worker.
  "name": "jotter-site",

  // Pinned, not "today". A compatibility date is how a Worker keeps the runtime
  // behaviour it was built against, so moving it should be a decision somebody
  // makes, never something that happens because a rebuild ran on a later date.
  "compatibility_date": "2026-08-25",

  // No "main" on purpose. This site has no Worker code of its own, and an
  // assets-only Worker is Cloudflare's own static-site pattern. A script here
  // would also stop the `_headers` that `scripts/finalize.mjs` writes applying
  // to anything that script answered: Cloudflare does not add those headers to
  // a response Worker code generated, so `_publish.json` would lose its
  // `Cache-Control: no-store` and a `noIndex` site its `X-Robots-Tag`.
  "assets": {
    // Where `npm run build` puts the site. Astro's default `outDir`, which
    // `astro.config.ts` does not override, and the directory that
    // `scripts/verify-build.mjs` and `scripts/finalize.mjs` both go on to read.
    // Kept in step with the build by a test, because the failure mode when
    // these drift is an empty site rather than an error.
    "directory": "./dist",

    // `src/pages/404.astro` is a real page, so every build writes `dist/404.html`
    // and a miss serves it: the "offers a way forward" page with recent notes on
    // it, rather than a bare status code. "none" would send an empty 404 to a
    // reader who mistyped a note name.
    "not_found_handling": "404-page",

    // Not the default, and the one line here that differs from the Quartz
    // starter's, because the two sites emit different URLs. All four producers
    // of a URL in this build (links, canonical, sitemap, search) spell a page
    // `/notes`, from `trailingSlash: 'never'`, and `build.format: 'file'`
    // writes it to `dist/notes.html`. "drop-trailing-slash" serves that file at
    // that address and sends `/notes/` back to it, which is also what cleans up
    // anything indexed at the trailing-slash form by an older build. The
    // default "auto-trailing-slash" reaches the same place from this file
    // layout, and this is the stricter of the two: it keeps its promise even if
    // `build.format` ever moves back, where "auto" would 307 every internal
    // link on the site to `/notes/`. "none" would 404 them.
    "html_handling": "drop-trailing-slash"
  }
}
